Help opens this manual inside Termic in a native MUI 5 window.
Use Chapter to select a topic, Contents to return here, Index for topics,
and Back to retrace your navigation. Previous and Next browse chapters.
Choose a Related topic and press Open to follow a link from this chapter.
Find and Find next search the displayed chapter. Escape closes help.
Resize the window as needed; small windows have native scrollbars.
The same file can also be opened separately in MultiView.
This manual does not execute commands. Type examples in an Amiga Shell.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Start "Getting started"
Termic uses MUI 5 native window controls. Drag the title to move it;
use the lower-right sizing gadget to resize, and the native zoom gadget
to enlarge or restore it. Closing saves the workspace first.
Native MUI tabs choose Agent, Editor, Build / CLI, Changes, Settings and Processors.
A compact selector also offers Projects, Tasks, Files and CLI panes.
Hover over an icon for its action name.
The source editor and Files retain light text on black.

1. Start TermicNative from its Workbench icon or the Shell.
2. Type a name in the project field; choose New project. A clean
   template is copied into a managed project drawer.
3. Select main.c in Files, or press F2 for the Editor.
4. Edit the program and press F8 (or Right-Amiga-S) to save.
5. Press F6 to build. Read the diagnostics in Build / CLI.
6. After a successful build, press F7 to run Program.

New task makes an independent copy of the selected workspace's source.
Projects uses one compact icon row: New, Rename and Import Folder.
The Tasks panel uses one compact icon row: New, Rename, Kanban, Queue
and Mark Done. Hover over each icon for its action name.
At small sizes, including 340x280, the selector shows one workspace pane.
At 800x500 and above, Projects/Tasks sit left, the tabbed work page sits
in the centre, and Files above CLI sit right. Instructions and agent
controls sit below the activity log, with the profile selector, YOLO and
four action icons together on one row beneath the instructions. The header
shows the version and Build, Run, Settings, About and Quit icons.
Files remains above CLI; drag its divider to change the height split.
Column dividers adjust widths. F1-F5 select the five work pages.
Long forms and tables have native scrollbars when needed. Resizing keeps
unsaved code, task instructions and the editor position.

You can build and edit locally without a cloud account. Direct API agents require an API key.
Codex on Mac uses the Mac's ChatGPT sign-in through the relay. Review generated changes before running them.

Next: @{"Projects" LINK Projects}  @{"Editor" LINK Editor}  @{"Build" LINK Build}
@ENDNODE

@NODE Install "Installation and dependencies"
Requirements: AmigaOS 3.2, a 68020 or newer CPU, Fast RAM, MUI 5.0
and TextEditor.mcc 15.56. Builds require VBCC, vasm, vlink and the NDK.
Termic displays this manual in its built-in MUI 5 help window.
MultiView is optional for viewing the AmigaGuide file outside Termic.

A complete local installation kit contains the executables, Source,
Template, SDK archives and installation scripts. The GitHub repository
contains source; GitHub releases include kits and pinned AmiPkg entries.

Unpack a kit into a new drawer. In an Amiga Shell, for example:

  Assign Termic: Work:TermicNative
  Execute Termic:InstallSDK
  Execute Termic:InstallUI
  Execute Termic:BuildNative
  Termic:TermicNative --icon Termic:TermicNative

Replace Work:TermicNative with your installation drawer. Double-click
TermicNative afterwards. It assigns Termic: to its own program drawer.
Use one installation at a time. InstallUI does not replace the MUI core.
Source files belong in Source; Termic.guide belongs beside TermicNative.

Cloud use needs a TCP/IP stack, AmiSSL and its CA certificates, a correct
clock and an OpenAI API key. Native Git was tested with AmiSSL 5.27.
Use the upstream AmiSSL installer. SDK checksums are in sdk-lock.json.

Read @{"Storage" LINK Storage} before selecting a drawer for Git projects.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Projects "Projects and independent tasks"
New project copies the clean Template into a managed project. The name
field supplies its label. Open... in the Project menu opens the source
import requester: choose a drawer to copy supported source into Termic.
This does not edit the original imported drawer.

New task copies the selected workspace's source and build command into
its own Tasks/task-NNNN drawer. It does not copy Git internals, credentials
or cloud conversation IDs. Tasks are source snapshots, not Git worktrees.

Select a task to switch its editor, instructions, logs and Git view.
Pending source/instruction edits are saved before switching or quitting.
A failed save prevents the switch. Rename changes the displayed label.
Mark done sets task status; it does not delete files or stop a worker.

Each task permits one worker. Up to 16 tasks can run workers concurrently.
A running task's editors are read-only. Other tasks remain available.
The last selected workspace is restored when Termic starts again.

See @{"Build" LINK Build}, @{"Agent" LINK Agent} and @{"Backup" LINK Storage}.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Editor "Source editor"
Select .c, .h, .txt or .md files in the Files pane. Create file uses the
filename field; use a simple name with one of these extensions.

C and header files have syntax colours on a black editor background.
Colours update after typing without replacing the text or undo history.
Saved files are plain source without display style codes. Ordinary Save
retains undo history and keeps the previous file as filename.bak.

Click the source text to edit code directly. The floppy icon beside Find/Next
saves your changes; F8, Right-Amiga-S and Project > Save also work. An asterisk
before the filename marks unsaved changes. A read-only caption and disabled
Save icon mean this workspace is busy or the file could not be loaded.
Use the Edit menu for Cut, Copy, Paste, Erase, Select All, Undo and Redo.
Menu actions target the focused text editor, including task instructions
and read-only logs (Copy only). Click in the intended text before editing.
String fields use their normal MUI editing keys. Unavailable editor menu
commands are ghosted. Find (magnifier) searches from the start; Find Next
(down arrow) finds a later hit. Hover over an icon for its action name.

Source files have a 32 KiB limit. Files containing literal ESC bytes or
unreadable/binary/oversize content are not editable. Instructions have an
8 KiB limit. A failed save leaves the previous file available.

See @{"Prompts and instructions" LINK Prompts}, @{"Clipboard" LINK Sharing} and @{"Keyboard shortcuts" LINK Menus}.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Build "Build and run"
Build (F6 or Right-Amiga-B) saves pending edits and invokes the native
worker in the selected workspace. Diagnostics appear in Build / CLI.
The default command compiles main.c with VBCC.

Settings contains a per-workspace build command. For several sources:

  vc +aos68k -cpu=68020 main.c helper.c -o Program.new -lamiga

Choose Save build command to store it. This is project data, separate
from application preferences. It must produce Program.new. Successful scripts
must reach EOF without Quit 0; early exits are rejected. BuildProject
promotes that output to Program only after the command succeeds.

A failed build preserves the last successful Program. Run after a failure
would therefore run the older executable. Check diagnostics before Run.
Run (F7 or Right-Amiga-R) explicitly launches Program. Generated programs
are not automatically run by the cloud agent's build tool.

Cancel job signals the worker; a running child application may need its
own Quit action before the worker can finish. See @{"Troubleshooting" LINK Trouble}.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Shell "CLI, ARexx and MicroPython"
The CLI panel directly below Files has a black editable command console,
a mode selector and a separate command field. Type after the final > prompt
and press Return to run a command directly in the console. Escape restores
the prompt if you edited the output above it. Only the final single-line
command is executable; output history and multiline pastes are not executed.
An unfinished command is preserved when new job output arrives. Switching
projects or tasks clears the draft. The console shows the last 32 KB of log;
the full log remains in Build / CLI and job.log. This is a command runner,
not an interactive terminal: programs that require stdin are not supported.
Choose CLI for
AmigaDOS commands, or ARexx / MicroPython for a script path and arguments.
Press Return in the separate field to run its text. The Execute/play button
runs the inline draft if nonempty, otherwise the separate field. Shift-F3
focuses the separate field directly. Commands run in the selected project or
task directory, shown below the mode selector.
The CLI directory name and file access use that same directory. Each command
starts there; a CD command does not change the next command's starting drawer.
Output is captured in the log.
The editor or agent tab stays visible while the command runs. The sidebar
shows the selected workspace's job output; Build / CLI shows the same log
at full size. The play and stop icons execute and cancel; hover for help.
Click in the output, then use Right-Amiga-A and Right-Amiga-C to copy it.

For example:
  List
  Version

ARexx example: select ARexx and enter Termic:Examples/hello.rexx.
Python example: select MicroPython and enter Termic:Examples/hello.py.
RexxMast must be running for ARexx; RX lives in SYS:Rexxc.
MicroPython lives in Termic:Tools/MicroPython; its license and provenance
are included there. It runs on the 68k CPU, with no ZZ9000 ARM offload.
Python packages written for desktop CPython may need adaptation.

The editor colours C/H, Python and ARexx. Agent and MCP source tools also
support text/Markdown; named guidance remains read-only to agents. No Termic-specific ARexx commands
are added to the application host port.

This is a command runner. Interactive
programs needing console input should be opened from a separate Shell.
Commands have the normal access of AmigaOS programs; there is no sandbox.

Use @{"Git" LINK Git} for local repository commands and @{"Build" LINK Build} for compilation.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Git "Git review and commits"
Git requires a filesystem supporting long filenames. Changes provides
Init repository, Status and Diff. Configure Git identity in the shell
before the first commit, using your own details:

  git config user.name "Your Name"
  git config user.email "user@example.com"

Enter a message and choose Stage source and commit. Return in the commit
field also submits it. Termic commits supported source; generated output
and task metadata are excluded in newly initialized repositories.
Existing .gitignore files are retained.

Tasks are independent snapshots, not branches/worktrees. Native Git's
other commands can be run explicitly in Build / CLI. Termic does not
push, fetch, publish a PR or contact a remote automatically.

See @{"Storage" LINK Storage} and @{"Limits" LINK Limits}.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Agent "Cloud agent"
In Settings choose a model available to your account and enter an API key.
Save persists preferences; Use applies them until reboot. A blank key
field retains the effective key and never displays it.

Enter instructions in the Agent tab and choose Start agent. The worker
sends the instructions and requested source/compiler output to OpenAI.
It can list/read/write C/H, Python, ARexx and text/Markdown files, build and execute.
Use system_info for live CPU, memory, OS/library versions and raw board IDs.
It leaves the machine model, CPU MHz and ARM availability unknown.
Use run_arexx for a saved .rexx/.rx script. Use build then run_program for C.
Edits invalidate the successful build; failed builds cannot run an old Program.
Execution returns success, actual exit_code, output and cancelled status.
Returned output is limited to 16 KiB; .command.log holds the full capture.
Runs wait for exit, without interactive stdin or arguments. Close GUI programs
to finish. Stop is cooperative; programs ignoring breaks can keep running.
The host tool-response wait is 180 seconds. Never replay an uncertain run.
Read-only profiles block writing, compilation and execution.
Choose New conversation after updating worker/proxy to load the new tools.
Long profile IDs use short session filenames for classic filesystems.
Existing short-ID conversations are retained. Long-ID profiles start a fresh
conversation after this filename update.
It has no general shell-command tool. File paths reject devices and
traversal. Each run defaults to eight requests; profiles allow a limit of 1-32.

Conversation IDs persist per task. New conversation clears the continuation
ID while retaining the transcript. Cancel requests a stop; network and
child-process cleanup can take time. API access and billing are required.

Keys are plaintext on classic AmigaOS: Config/api-key for saved defaults,
ENV:Termic/api-key for temporary use. Do not share these files or include
them in backups intended for publication. Keys are not command arguments.
TLS verifies certificates and hostnames. Test verified HTTPS checks the
unauthenticated connection (HTTP 401), not account or model access.

Authenticated cloud use still needs verification with your own account.
See @{"Preferences" LINK Prefs}, @{"Troubleshooting" LINK Trouble} and @{"Limits" LINK Limits}.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Prefs "Application preferences"
Open Settings > Preferences or press F5. Set the cloud model, optional
API key and project drawer. The key field is always blank when restored.
The project drawer accepts letters, digits, colon, slash, underscore and
hyphen. Restart Termic after changing the project drawer.

The bottom buttons are ordered Save, Use, Cancel:

Save   Applies values and writes defaults in Termic:Config plus the
       current ENV:Termic overrides. New workers use the new values.
Use    Writes ENV:Termic only; saved defaults remain unchanged. The
       values survive restarting Termic but disappear at system reboot.
Cancel Discards unapplied preference edits and returns to the Agent tab.
       It cannot undo a build command already saved for the workspace.

Temporary ENV values are read first, then the installation Config drawer.
When absent, the UI supplies its built-in model and project drawer.
A blank API key preserves the effective key; Save also persists a key
previously applied using Use. A failure may leave some settings written;
check storage and retry if Termic reports a preference write failure.

Build commands have their own Save build command button because they are
workspace data. Save preferences stores the general settings. These two
labelled Save buttons share the same width and left alignment.
Settings > MUI Settings... opens MUI's appearance editor.
Helvetica 13 controls and Courier 13 source text match the reference UI.
Missing disk fonts fall back to the corresponding MUI font roles. Framed
groups and labelled fields organize preferences. MUI supplies colours,
window controls and gadget styling. Source text retains the black background
and readable syntax palette. System preferences are not changed.

Preset files and SETTINGS ToolTypes/CLI overrides are not implemented.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Menus "Menus and keyboard shortcuts"
Hold the right mouse button to access the normal Amiga menu strip.
Project and Edit come first; Settings is next to the rightmost Help menu.
Separators group commands and unavailable actions are ghosted.
Common buttons use icons with hover tooltips: floppy Save, triangle Run,
square Stop, hammer Build, gear Settings, magnifier Find, speech bubble
Prompts and keyboard Shortcuts. Dialog Save/Use/Cancel use floppy/check/cross.
The menu names and keyboard commands remain descriptive.

Project: New, Open... (import source drawer), Save, About..., Quit.
Edit: Cut, Copy, Paste, Erase, Select All, Undo, Redo.
Workspace: New Task, Mark Done, Build, Run, Start Agent, Cancel Job,
           Git Status, Git Diff.
View: Agent, Editor, Build / CLI, Changes, Activity / processes...
Tools: Process Monitor... (also available from View > Activity / processes...).
Right Amiga I opens Activity. See @{"Process monitor" LINK activitymonitor}.
Settings: Preferences, MUI Settings..., Save Settings, Use Settings,
          Cancel Edits (the last three apply while Settings is open).
Help: Contents... opens this manual in the native MUI 5 help window.
      Keyboard shortcuts opens the black reference of active bindings.

@{"Prompt library and project instructions" LINK Prompts}
Right Amiga P opens Prompts; Right Amiga K opens keyboard shortcuts.
Right Amiga E opens Agents / CLI / Scripts; Right Amiga M opens MCP settings.
Right Amiga I opens Tools > Process Monitor from any workspace page.
Default prompt keys: Tab sends and Shift Tab changes agent profile. Ctrl J adds
a newline, Ctrl G opens the editor, Ctrl R searches history and F4 opens
the Activity process monitor. Two Esc presses recall the last sent message. In an empty prompt,
/ opens commands, @ mentions the open file, ! opens CLI, ? shows shortcuts.
Ctrl T focuses transcript. F3 then finds text, PgUp/PgDn scroll, and
Ctrl Home/End or Shift Alt comma/period go to top/latest. Ctrl Space starts
line selection; Up/Down extends; Ctrl Space finishes; Right Amiga C copies.
Esc returns to prompt. Ctrl C quits from prompt/transcript with save handling.
These keys do not replace editor Tab. Image paste, reasoning adjustment,
and subagent switching are not available.
Configure... in Keyboard shortcuts remaps 16 prompt/transcript actions.
Select an action and binding, then Assign. Conflicts are rejected. Disabled
frees a binding. Save persists Config/keymap.json with backup; Use lasts this
session; Cancel discards the draft. Defaults still needs Save or Use.
Menu keys, editor bindings, Escape and empty-prompt symbols stay fixed.

Right-Amiga + N New project     O Open/import    S Save
Right-Amiga + X Cut             C Copy           V Paste
Right-Amiga + Z Undo            Q Quit           B Build
Right-Amiga + R Run             , Preferences
Right-Amiga + 1 Agent           2 Editor         3 Build / CLI
Right-Amiga + 4 Changes

F1 Agent; F2 Editor; F3 Build / CLI; F4 Changes; F5 Settings.
F6 Build; F7 Run; F8 Save; F9 New task; F10 Quit.
Shift-F1 Start agent; Shift-F2 New filename; Shift-F3 Shell field;
Shift-F4 Commit field; Shift-F5 Git init; Shift-F6 Git status;
Shift-F7 Git diff; Shift-F8 Mark done; Shift-F9 New project;
Shift-F10 Cancel job. Return submits filename, command or commit fields.
Help opens the manual; Shift-Help opens About. Escape closes About.
MUI single-line fields consume some shortcuts for text editing. Tab out
to a button before using application keys; mouse menus remain available.

MagicMenu is an optional system menu enhancer. Termic uses standard MUI
menu strips and does not embed, configure or require MagicMenu. Its
runtime interaction has not yet been verified. Workbench's Tools menu
is separate from this application's menu strip.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Workbench "Workbench menus: ToolsDaemon and MagicMenu"
Termic's Project/Edit menus belong to its application window. Workbench's
Tools menu and additional ToolsDaemon menus belong to Workbench.

For an existing ToolsDaemon installation, open ToolsPrefs and add a
Development menu title (Menu checkbox). Drag the TermicNative Workbench
icon into its window to create a launcher, then move it under that title.
A Workbench command uses the stack size from Termic's generated icon
(262144 bytes). Choose an unused key if you want a launcher shortcut.

To add a User guide entry, create a Shell/CLI command such as:

  SYS:Utilities/MultiView Work:TermicNative/Termic.guide

Use the real installation path for both entries. Termic: is assigned when
Termic starts; an absolute path lets a Workbench launcher work before it
has started. ToolsPrefs supports menu items, sub-items and separators.
Use its editor to merge entries into your own configuration, then save.
Do not replace your existing menu file with an example configuration.

ToolsDaemon 2.1a is a separate optional commodity, with ToolsPrefs and
ToolsDaemon-handler. Its supplied documentation explains installation.
Termic does not install it into WBStartup or modify S:ToolsDaemon.menu.
Its behavior on this Workbench 3.2.3 system is not yet verified.

MagicMenu is a different optional tool: it changes how native menus look
and appear. Termic uses MUI's native Intuition menu strip, without a custom
menu renderer or an embedded MagicMenu patch. Configure appearance in
MagicMenu itself if already installed. Runtime compatibility is unverified.

Upstream references:
https://aminet.net/package/util/boot/ToolsDaemon21a
https://github.com/jens-maus/magicmenu

@{"Application menus" LINK Menus}  @{"Preferences" LINK Prefs}
@ENDNODE

@NODE Sharing "Clipboard and data sharing"
Termic uses TextEditor.mcc's normal Amiga clipboard for Cut, Copy and
Paste. TextEditor exchanges IFF FTXT text through clipboard.device,
allowing text to move between Termic and other clipboard-aware apps.
It is not a private Termic-only copy buffer.

Select text in an editor or log, then Edit > Copy (Right-Amiga-C).
Switch to a writable editor and Paste (Right-Amiga-V). Cut is available
only for writable text. In single-line fields use MUI's normal keys.
Pasted source does not inherit clipboard colours; saved source is plain
text. Compilers continue to consume ordinary .c/.h files.

Use source-drawer import to copy external source into a managed project.
There is no IFF project container, live file notification/reload service,
ARexx application port, AppWindow drop target or clipboard unit selector.
The internal TextEditor ARexx command interface is not an application port.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Storage "Storage and backups"
Select a long-filename filesystem (PFS3/SFS or suitable FFS) for Git.
The optional TermicStorage helper can create a fresh 64 MiB image:

  Termic:TermicStorage --create

It refuses an existing Projects.hdf or a device unit owned by another
image. It initializes only its new image, never a physical partition.
This optional fresh-create path has not yet been hardware-tested.
Unit 7 of diskimage.device is reserved for it. If already in use, select
a different long-filename project drawer in Settings.

To back up an existing image, finish jobs and leave all TMGIT: directories
in other applications and Shells. From a Shell outside TMGIT:, run:

  Termic:TermicStorage --eject

After successful ejection, copy Projects.hdf to backup storage. Remount:

  Termic:TermicStorage

Do not force eject a busy volume. Also back up application preferences
privately; Config/api-key contains a secret. Source saves keep one .bak
copy. A .bak is not a substitute for a separate disk backup.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Trouble "Troubleshooting"
No main window: check MUI 5.0 and TextEditor.mcc installation. Run from
Shell for startup messages, using Stack 262144 before launching TermicNative.
The Workbench icon already requests this stack. Keep supporting files together.
MUI 5 uses Title-class tabs; MUI 3.8 uses its original Register tabs.

Help fails: install an intact Termic.guide beside TermicNative.
The built-in reader needs MUI 5 and TextEditor.mcc, like the workspace.
MultiView and the AmigaGuide datatype are needed only for external viewing.

Cannot save: check free space, protection, file size and active jobs.
Never overwrite the original with the explanatory read-only error text.

Build fails: inspect Build / CLI, VBCC setup and the workspace command.
It must write Program.new. The previous successful Program is retained.

Git fails: check long-filename support, AmiSSL runtime and Git identity.

HTTPS fails: check network, DNS, system clock, CA files and AmiSSL.
HTTP 401 in the unauthenticated test is expected. Authentication/model
access and billing are separate checks for an actual agent run.

Workspace remains busy: inspect job.log and running tasks. A child program
may need its own Quit action. Remove a stale .busy drawer only after
confirming its worker has stopped. Never remove an active worker's lock.

Preference changes disappear: Use is temporary until reboot. Choose Save
for persistent defaults. A changed project drawer requires app restart.

Source colours look old: release a selection and pause typing briefly.
Live C/H, Python and ARexx colours refresh while preserving Undo.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Limits "Limits and hardware"
Source file: 32 KiB. Task instructions: 8 KiB. HTTP response: 256 KiB.
Agent compiler output: 16 KiB. File list: 128 files, six directory levels.
Workers: 16 per application, one per task. Agent rounds: 1-32 per profile (default eight).
Network deadline: 120 seconds after DNS; DNS timing belongs to the stack.
Large imports copy synchronously and can delay the interface.

Test hardware: A4000TX, TF4060/68060, Workbench 3.2.3, MUI 5.0,
TextEditor.mcc 15.56, AmiSSL 5.27 and a ZZ9000 RTG display.
Compilation, TLS and file processing run on the 68k. The ZZ9000 supplies
RTG graphics; ARM compute offload is not implemented. Termic does not
change firmware, display mode or ownership of ARM applications.

This is a native MUI implementation, not the React/Tauri desktop app.
No native desktop agent CLIs, interactive PTY or Git worktrees.
Host profiles provide buffered live response text, worktrees and PR controls.
The queue supports independent workers and broadcast task copies. Host proxy
requests stay serial; race mode is not implemented.
Classic AmigaOS has no process isolation: review code before running it.
@ENDNODE

@NODE About "Version, support and credits"
Termic Amiga 0.2.0 Beta. Native version source: Source/version.h.
Project > About... and the About toolbar button show version and credits.
AmigaDOS can inspect all three executable version tags:

  Version Termic:TermicNative FILE FULL
  Termic:TermicNative --version

Source and support: https://github.com/SkiltonUSA/Termic-Amiga
Original desktop project: https://github.com/simion/termic
Amiga adaptation: Dominic Skilton; development assistance: Codex.
Original Termic: Simion Agavriloaei and contributors.

Thanks to MUI, TextEditor.mcc, VBCC, vasm, vlink, NDK, AmiSSL, cJSON,
native Git and Amiga MCP contributors. GNU AGPL v3 or later applies to
Termic; third-party components retain their licences. See LICENSE.

Manual structure follows AmigaGuide 101. Interface conventions draw on
the AmigaOS UI Style Guide's Menus, Preferences and Data Sharing chapters:
https://wiki.amigaos.net/wiki/AmigaGuide_101
https://wiki.amigaos.net/wiki/AmigaOS_Manual:_Workbench_Fonts
https://wiki.amigaos.net/wiki/UI_Style_Guide_Menus
https://wiki.amigaos.net/wiki/UI_Style_Guide_Preferences
https://wiki.amigaos.net/wiki/UI_Style_Guide_Data_Sharing
@ENDNODE

