@ENDNODE

@NODE Profiles "Agents, CLI and scripts"
Settings > Agents / CLI / Scripts opens the profile manager.
The supplied profiles are OpenAI API and Amiga CLI. Desktop agent CLIs
are not native Amiga programs and are not bundled.

Add API, Add CLI, Add ARexx, Add MicroPython, Duplicate and Remove
manage profiles.
Built-ins can be edited and reset, but not removed. Reset keeps credentials
and custom profiles. All / agents / CLI and Scripts filters the list.
Save persists Config and ENV; Use updates ENV until reboot; Cancel discards
draft edits. Select an applied profile on the Agent page before starting.

General: name, type, launcher visibility, model, API key and source access.
Blank keys retain that profile's existing credential. Duplicates need their
own key. Blank models use the main Settings model. Keys remain plaintext
in local Config/ENV files, separate from the profile registry.

Launch / Sessions: native command, default/YOLO/resume arguments, resume
switch, API step limit (1-32) and output token limit (256-16384).
The launcher shows YOLO Off or YOLO On. On adds the native profile's YOLO
arguments; API and proxy profiles ignore this toggle. Native arguments
use AmigaDOS syntax. API sessions are separated by task and profile.
New conversation resets the selected profile's session.

Environment: native command variables, one NAME=value per line. These are
worker-local and do not alter ENV:. Native commands have normal AmigaDOS
access. API read-only access rejects edits and builds locally.

Status: native workers report running, completion and failure. Optional
API usage reports actual input/output tokens, not plan quotas. External
CLI hooks, live YOLO switching, Keychain roles and Docker are unavailable.

@{"Cloud agent" LINK Agent}  @{"Preferences" LINK Prefs}  @{"MCP" LINK MCP}
@ENDNODE

@NODE MCP "Amiga MCP and CLI access"
Settings > Agent access / MCP enables native MCP tools. Save persists the
setting; Use lasts until reboot. Default is disabled. Disabling access
rejects new calls without cancelling workers already started.

In a Shell, a local MCP client can start:
  Termic:TermicMCP --stdio

One JSON-RPC message is read/written per line. Scripts can instead use:
  Termic:TermicMCP --request request.json --reply reply.json

Clients on another computer use scripts/amiga/mcp_bridge.py from the
repository and a Python environment with the mcp package. The connector
uses the existing DevBench bridge; it opens no new network listener.
Register its stdio command in the MCP client's settings. Automatic desktop
peer discovery, pairing codes and agent registration are not implemented.

Routines: project_list, task_list, task_new, task_status, task_send,
task_stop, task_result, task_log, task_build, agent_list, file_read,
file_write, scratchpad_write, scratchpad_read and scratchpad_list.

Task routines take project and task drawer IDs. task_new copies sources
without launching an agent. task_send starts a direct API profile.
Writes require idle MCP-created tasks that are not selected in the editor.
File tools edit task C/H files and read named project guidance; keys and arbitrary shell commands
are not exposed. Scratchpads live in .scratchpads, excluded from new-task
Git tracking. Read them through MCP; there is no scratchpad editor tab yet.

The task list refreshes on native MCP/worker notifications without reloading
the current editor. See docs/agents-and-mcp.md in the repository for the
client configuration example and protocol compatibility details.

@{"Agent profiles" LINK Profiles}  @{"Projects" LINK Projects}  @{"Contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE Prompts "Prompts and project instructions"
Open Prompts beside the composer, Settings > Prompts, or Right Amiga P.
The 17 defaults come from desktop Termic, with Amiga tool-limit guidance.
Review, Deep review, Write tests, Security review, Explain the changes,
Commit, Commit and push, Fix merge conflicts, Verify end to end,
Fix the bug, Work on the issue, Simplify, What is the state?,
Continue from last, Hand off to another agent, Update the docs,
and Research first are included.

Edit titles and bodies. New/Duplicate add prompts; Remove, Reset selected,
Restore missing defaults, Up/Down and Enabled manage the list.
Save persists in Termic:Config/prompts.json and ENV:Termic/prompts.json.
Use lasts until reboot; Cancel discards library edits.
Insert + Use appends to the task composer and uses library edits until
reboot. It does not send. Select the agent profile and press Tab to send.
The composer holds 8192 bytes. Disabled prompts cannot be inserted.

These are instructions for agents, not executable scripts. Native API tools
cannot run Git, push, browse, manage PRs or dispatch subagents. Upstream
shell examples need a capable external agent and AmigaDOS adaptation.

Workspace > Install agent instructions adds missing files to the active
project/task, preserving existing edits:
  AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md
  .agents/skills/termic/SKILL.md and .termic-version
  .agents/skills/termic/references/development.md and mcp.md

New projects include these files and new tasks copy them. The Files list
shows them for editing. API requests load current AGENTS.md and SKILL.md
(up to 8192 bytes each). Missing or unreadable files are skipped. Agent/MCP
file tools can read the six named files; C/H and ARexx files can be written by agents.
CLAUDE.md is a compatibility pointer, not an installed agent executable.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Codex "Codex, Claude and Cursor through AI Proxy"
Use host AI sign-in from the Amiga task composer.
No API key or sign-in token is copied to the Amiga.

Open Termic AI Proxy on macOS or Windows 11. The proxy connects automatically
when opened. Its window provides Start, Stop, Sign in and connection status.
Install the provider CLI and keep the existing DevBench MCP bridge running.
The proxy is not the serial bridge. Closing it requests an orderly stop.
No login item or system startup service is installed.

On the Amiga, open Settings > Agents / CLI / Scripts and choose Add Codex,
Add Claude or Add Cursor. Save, select the profile, enter a task and press Tab.
Enter a supported Model identifier, or leave it blank for the host default.
The on Mac profile names also work with a Windows proxy.
Choose read-only or edit/build/run access. API steps / relay calls limits
native tool calls. Token/usage and CLI fields do not apply to proxy profiles.
Provider subscription access and usage limits still apply.

The relay provides C/H/ARexx files, backup saves, native builds/execution
and live hardware queries. Project guidance
is included. Responses appear progressively in the transcript while generated.
The proxy combines small text chunks; bridge speed affects update frequency.
Tool start and return markers show the current activity. Build / CLI and the
CLI panel show native tool activity without waiting for the whole agent turn.
Replies wrap to the transcript width without changing the saved text.
Updated host proxies and workers negotiate alternating reply files to
avoid shell cleanup for each message. Older peers remain compatible.
Task pickup polls every half-second; live text batches every 0.2 seconds.
These improve bridge responsiveness, not the model's thinking speed.

Activity opens at the latest line and follows new output automatically.
Select text to hold it for copying. Release selection to catch up to the bottom.
The Agent page combines cyan [Agent] responses and amber [Log] worker
messages in one black activity pane. Live entries appear in polling order;
opening a saved task shows its transcript followed by its latest job log.
Scrollback and selected text stay stable while updates arrive. The task
instruction box sits above one row containing the profile selector, YOLO
control and four action buttons.
The log comes from local job.log. Mac proxy diagnostics remain in the
AI Proxy app when the bridge cannot deliver them to the Amiga.
The direct API profile does not stream tokens.
One relay task runs at a time. Stop (Shift F10) cancels; New conversation starts
a separate thread. The host keeps conversation history and credentials.
After changing file access, start a new conversation to refresh available tools.
Cursor uses await_operation to collect results in polls of up to 20 seconds.
The operation continues between polls. Polls do not repeat execution or count
against the native tool limit. A running operation is not a successful result.
Stop during native builds/file I/O preserves task-lock and port cleanup.

If the host is unavailable, the worker times out. Start the proxy and send again.
A failed conversation resume needs New conversation. Tasks are not retried
automatically. Custom Amiga build commands retain ordinary AmigaOS access.
The general MCP access setting governs external clients; a relay started by
the user does not require it. Codex app-server is experimental.

Desktop setup, downloads and verification: docs/ai-proxy.md in the source repo.
The optional command-line launcher remains available for developers.

See also: @{"Profiles" LINK Profiles}  @{"MCP" LINK MCP}  @{"Cloud agent" LINK Agent}
@ENDNODE

@NODE TaskFlow "Kanban board and persistent queue"
Right Amiga Y opens the board: Backlog, Needs attention, Working, Review,
Done and Archived. Search task/project names and press Return. Select a
task to open it, queue work, review, mark done, archive or restore it.
Running jobs determine Working. Agent completion waits in Review for you.
Drag idle tasks between columns or use the buttons. A drop saves state;
it never starts work. Working accepts only actual jobs. Queued/running
tasks cannot move; failed saves leave the previous state intact.

Right Amiga J opens the queue. Add agent instructions or builds, reorder
pending rows, remove rows or clear finished history. Parallel work permits
one to four independent workers (default one). Host proxy requests stay
serial. A blocked head entry waits; later entries are not moved ahead.
A failure pauses new launches; active jobs can finish. Resume continues without retrying the failed
entry. Re-add a failed task explicitly to retry it. Pause prevents the next
launch; use Stop for active work. Queued tasks are reserved against edits.

The queue survives in Config/queue.json and starts paused after a restart.
Existing workers are reattached; uncertain interrupted jobs need attention.
They are never replayed automatically. Up to 64 queue entries are retained.

Queue > Broadcast... copies the saved idle source task for each selected
agent profile. Shift-click selects several profiles. Enter a shared prompt,
then Queue copies. Each copy has separate source, instructions and history;
YOLO is off. Dispatch pauses so you can inspect the copies before Resume.
A partial storage failure reports how many copies were queued. Inspect any
unfinished copies before retrying. Broadcast does not start jobs by itself.

@{"Review and notes" LINK ReviewNotes}  @{"Projects" LINK Projects}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ReviewNotes "Agent edit review and scratchpads"
Right Amiga U opens agent edit review. Unified diff uses a black background:
green + additions, red - removals and blue headers. View also offers
Before / after. Large changes use replacement alignment to bound work on
68k; all lines remain visible. Missing final newlines are marked.
Accept/Revert applies to the whole file. Accept keeps the edit. Revert asks for confirmation, restores the original
and retains generated contents in .bak. A new file moves to .bak. Later
user edits cause a conflict and are never overwritten by review.

The journal covers agent write_file/write_source tools, not external
editors, MCP file writes or ARexx/program side effects. Use Git diff for
those. Resolving edits does not automatically mark a task Done.

Right Amiga L opens scratchpads. Name a note with letters, digits, - or _,
choose New note, edit and Save. Notes share .scratchpads storage with MCP.
Switching notes and closing save changes. A failed save keeps notes open.
Each note is limited to 32 KiB; busy/queued tasks cannot save notes.

C/H, Python and ARexx have live lexical colours. Recolouring preserves Undo
and waits while text is selected. Compiler locations below Build / CLI
output open source at the reported line on double-click.
The run_python agent tool executes task .py files with native MicroPython.

@{"Task board" LINK TaskFlow}  @{"Source editor" LINK Editor}
@ENDNODE

@NODE HostGit "Git branches and host worktrees"
Right Amiga G opens native List/Create/Switch branch controls. Initialize
the task repository first. Checkout uses normal Git protection, no force.

Host worktrees require updated Termic AI Proxy, Git and authenticated gh
on Mac or Windows. Enter GitHub owner/repository, task branch, base branch
and title. Worktree status prepares and inspects a managed host worktree.
Sync and commit copies supported Amiga source files to the task branch.
Dirty host worktrees are refused. Missing Amiga files are not host deletes.

Create PR asks before syncing, committing, pushing and creating/reusing a
GitHub pull request. Credentials remain on the host. These operations do
not use AI. Stop cancels host processes. Failed work is not reset/retried.
Native amiga-git itself does not support worktrees.

@{"Git review" LINK Git}  @{"Task board" LINK TaskFlow}
@ENDNODE

@NODE activitymonitor "Activity / Process Monitor"
@{b}Activity / Process Monitor@{ub}

Tools > Process Monitor... or Right Amiga I selects the Processors tab.
F4 also selects it when the agent prompt has focus.
ZZ9000... shows optional board/ARM information; Hide ZZ9000 closes that panel.
It starts hidden and does not query the board while hidden. This button does
not enable ARM compute offload. Unsupported metrics remain unavailable.
The resizable monitor is part of the main Termic window; jobs and editing
continue. The Agent button returns to the conversation. Hiding the tab stops
sampling; a previously confirmed service restart continues to be supervised.

CPU is a statistical VBlank estimate, refreshed every second. Memory shows
partial tracked allocations, not total heap or RSS. The summary shows global
Chip/Fast free and total memory. Task address identifies an Exec task; it is
not a Unix PID. OS3 uptime and output counters are unavailable.

Sort by Address for stable rows, CPU or Memory descending, or Name ascending.
Selection follows the same task across sorting and refreshes. Pause freezes
updates and stops CPU sampling; Resume starts a new sample interval.
A confirmed service restart continues while the display is paused.

Stop selected asks for confirmation and sends cooperative CTRL-C. Protected
system/Termic tasks and stale identities are refused. There is no forced kill.
Restart service uses optional services.txt beside TermicNative. Definitions
are never executed on load. See the bundled process-monitor README for syntax.
Quitting Termic cancels pending restart supervision.

ZZ9000 AutoConfig boards and the optional ARM owner port are reported.
ARM process/load/memory/dependency metrics remain unavailable through the
current monitor interface. Shared RAM size does not measure ARM heap usage.
No firmware is changed and an absent owner port does not establish ARM idle.
@ENDNODE

@NODE TINY "Offline Tiny agent"
TINY 2.1 by WollyMonjac, using the tiny68k Amiga port.
Select Tiny 2.1, model tiny-2.1, beneath Task instructions.
Tiny is the default for tasks without an explicit saved agent selection.
Existing task selections are preserved.

Ask one short question (up to 72 printable ASCII characters).
The answer appears in Agent activity. Stop uses normal task cancellation.
No network, API key, host proxy or ZZ9000 is needed. No automatic cloud fallback.
Runtime: Termic:Tools/Tiny/tiny68k. Model: Termic:Tools/Tiny/tiny.t68.
LOCAL ASSISTANT COMMANDS
Enter these in Task instructions with Tiny selected, then Start Agent:

/help                 Show available commands.
/docs yolo            Search the installed guide, with chapter/source labels.
/files                List project sources.
/read main.c          Read a project file; no changes are made.
/history              Show saved recent questions, answers and notes.
/recall MUI           Find matching saved context.
/remember Use MUI 5   Save a note for this task.
/forget               Reset Tiny memory; retain the activity transcript.
/agents               List configured host-agent IDs.
/handoff ID request   Send a request and saved context to that host agent.

Replace ID with an ID shown by /agents. Handoff supports Codex, Claude and
Cursor profiles through Termic AI Proxy on Mac or Windows. Start the proxy
and sign in to the selected provider first. Handoff is the only command
above requiring a host connection. There is no automatic provider fallback.
Target profile model, permissions, step limits and resume settings apply.
Choose a read-only target to keep the handoff read-only too.
Replies appear in the same activity transcript; Tiny stays selected.
After success, memory retains its last 1024 characters for later recall.
The original task instructions are preserved. Stop cancels the relay job.

READ-ONLY PROJECT ACCESS
Read C/H, Python, ARexx, text/Markdown or named project guidance files up to
32 KiB. Paths must stay inside this task/project. Device paths, traversal,
reserved task/session files and links outside the project are rejected.
Tiny cannot write files, run builds or execute code.

LOOKUP AND MEMORY
/docs is keyword search, returning the best matching chapter from this
installed guide. Try kanban, shortcuts or ARexx. Results are source text,
not model-generated answers. Excerpts are limited to 12,000 characters;
open Help for the complete chapter. Ordinary questions still use Tiny.

Each task stores .tiny-memory.json: the last 8 turns, with up to 256
characters per question and 1024 per answer, plus 4096 characters of notes.
Memory survives closing Termic and restarting. Full activity is retained
in transcript.txt up to its existing 256 KiB limit. /history and /recall
do not replace saved turns. Corrupt memory is reported; /forget resets it.

Handoff sends your new request (up to 3000 characters), up to 512 characters
of notes and the newest complete saved turns fitting 4096 characters.
Previously read file excerpts can be included. Check /history first.
The receiving agent is told that saved context is reference data and Tiny
answers need verification. No credentials are included by these commands.

/forget resets memory and removes its normal backup. It does not erase the
activity transcript, external backups or conversations on the host.
This memory supports recall and handoff; it does not enlarge Tiny's model
context. The neural model still answers only the current short question.
Use /recall explicitly, or hand off when conversation reasoning is needed.

Keep Tools/Tiny/LICENSE and DATA_LICENSES.md with the model.
Upstream source and training kit: https://github.com/WollyMonjac/Tiny-2.1
Amiga inference port: https://github.com/SkiltonUSA/tiny68k
Training is a separate host task; the bundled model is ready for offline use.
The optional ZZ9000 details button does not offload Tiny inference.
@ENDNODE
